backdrop displays the festival name in large ornate lettering above a golden Ukrainian trident (Tryzub) symbol, with stage lighting rigs visible overhead.\n\nFrom 119s through 137s, the video transitions to a static, detailed **linguistic map of Ukraine** that remains on screen for the duration. The map uses color-coding to illustrate dialect regions: **red** for Southwestern dialects (western Ukraine), **yellow** for Southeastern dialects (eastern/central Ukraine), and **blue** for Northern Polissian dialects (northwestern border regions). A comprehensive legend identifies numbered sub-dialect regions (1-20), transitional zones with patterned overlays, and surrounding countries (Poland, Belarus, Russia, Romania, Moldova) plus the Black Sea. Geographic labels include major cities like Kyiv, Lviv, and Odesa.\n\nThe mood shifts from energetic cultural celebration to **educational analysis**, suggesting the map serves as a visual aid for narration about Ukraine's linguistic geography and dialect diversity. The extended static display of the map (approximately 20 seconds) indicates a documentary-style explanatory segment following the introductory cultural footage.", 'similarity score': 0.40662655234336853}, {'clip_52_60': 'clip_52_60', 'text': 'The sequence transitions between two static informational graphics with no visible movement or characters:\n\n**Frames 52s–53s:** A political map of Eastern Europe and the Black Sea region is displayed. Ukraine is highlighted in purple, surrounded by labeled neighboring countries including Poland, Belarus, Slovakia, Hungary, Romania, Moldova, Serbia, Bulgaria, Greece, and Turkey. The Black Sea is visible to the southeast.\n\n**Frames 54s–60s:** The view switches to a vertical bar chart titled "Composition of Ukraine by ethnicity," citing the 2001 Census. The chart displays ethnic breakdowns: Ukrainians (77.8%), Russians (17.3%), Romanians and Moldovans (0.8%), Belarusians (0.6%), Crimean Tatars (0.5%), Bulgarians (0.4%), Hungari